1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It's a legendary part of the American past, the job that created the cowboy.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:14,000
It's the heart of the food chain, the business of eating and being eaten.

3
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:26,000
It's as old as farming and as current as cloning, an industry in which a single bull can sire a hundred thousand calves.

4
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:31,000
Now, Cattle Ranches on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:45,360
The image of cattle ranching in America

6
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:48,860
is still the traditional one, full of open spaces,

7
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:53,860
big skies, large herds, and cowboys.

8
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:00,060
But cattle ranching today isn't really about cowboys and countryside.

9
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:02,600
It's about meat.

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:11,900
The very serious and intensely competitive business of putting some 25 billion pounds of steak, barbecue and hamburger on the national grill each year.

11
00:01:12,780 --> 00:01:16,040
Never have the economic stresses and demands been so great.

12
00:01:17,100 --> 00:01:23,300
Never have ranchers been forced to speed up and streamline the very cycles of nature to push their cattle quite so hard,

13
00:01:23,301 --> 00:01:27,140
to grow them so big, so fast, so meaty, so uniform,

14
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:31,740
using everything from sonograms that analyze the genetic quality of an animal's flesh

15
00:01:31,741 --> 00:01:36,420
to computer chips that carry growth and weight statistics stretching back for generations

16
00:01:36,421 --> 00:01:44,300
to fertility pills and embryo transplants that enable a single cow to conceive as many as 36 calves.

17
00:01:47,560 --> 00:01:51,580
All this in an effort to make cattle ranching as efficient as an assembly line

18
00:01:51,581 --> 00:01:55,220
and to woo consumers with consistently tender stakes.

19
00:01:57,420 --> 00:02:00,460
In cattle ranching, the Old West is butting up hard

20
00:02:00,461 --> 00:02:02,740
against the 21st century bottom line.

21
00:02:04,560 --> 00:02:08,600
Used to be you'd put a cow and bull together,

22
00:02:08,720 --> 00:02:10,660
have a calf, sell a calf, make money.

23
00:02:10,800 --> 00:02:11,740
It's not like that anymore.

24
00:02:12,520 --> 00:02:14,320
It's pretty high-tech.

25
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:18,980
You've got to do it correctly or you're out of business.

26
00:02:21,580 --> 00:02:28,660
Meet Broadway, a prized Simmental bull with a kind of genes that cattle ranchers around the world have come to covet.

27
00:02:30,100 --> 00:02:35,300
Broadway is a seed stock bull, which means he isn't destined for the slaughterhouse like most cattle.

28
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:43,020
Instead, he'll spend his life siring hundreds, perhaps thousands, and possibly tens of thousands of calves.

29
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:47,560
Calves with the right stuff from both a consumer and an industrial point of view.

30
00:02:48,440 --> 00:02:51,360
Broadway won't actually mate with tens of thousands of cows.

31
00:02:51,580 --> 00:02:59,680
Because of his superior genetic makeup, his semen will be collected and sold in straws over the Internet for $200 apiece,

32
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:05,580
and then used via artificial insemination in ranches as far away as South America, Europe, and Australia.

33
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:11,720
This is a good example of a high-quality red Brangus bull.

34
00:03:12,140 --> 00:03:15,020
This particular bull has been leased by American Breeder Service.

35
00:03:15,021 --> 00:03:18,220
There's been a tremendous amount of semen collected on this bull,

36
00:03:18,221 --> 00:03:21,660
and the semen sales on this bull look very good in the future.

37
00:03:22,620 --> 00:03:27,380
Top bulls can generate as much as $150,000 a year in semen sales.

38
00:03:28,100 --> 00:03:33,020
Super bulls with extraordinarily desirable traits can sire upwards of 100,000 calves,

39
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:36,700
so many that they'll actually alter the genetic makeup of their breed.

40
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:41,260
In other words, the entire breed will take on the traits of a single sire.

41
00:03:41,261 --> 00:03:49,540
You just don't hardly pick up an Angus pedigree today that doesn't go back to the traveler

42
00:03:49,541 --> 00:03:51,020
bull or the Scotch cap bull.

43
00:03:51,480 --> 00:03:55,520
I mean, those bulls were used extremely heavy for many, many years.

44
00:03:56,420 --> 00:04:01,720
An increasingly important part of cattle ranching in the 21st century takes place in the lab,

45
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:02,820
not the ranch.

46
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:09,620
Elgin Breeders in Elgin, Texas, has been collecting and storing bull semen since 1954.

47
00:04:09,621 --> 00:04:12,660
This is the semen storage room.

48
00:04:12,661 --> 00:04:16,380
There's approximately 5 million straws of semen in these tanks.

49
00:04:16,381 --> 00:04:19,580
This represents almost 40 years' worth of collection activity.

50
00:04:30,120 --> 00:04:33,380
A single tank holds semen samples for a thousand bowls.

51
00:04:35,240 --> 00:04:39,120
These tanks are exactly like a large coffee thermos.

52
00:04:39,121 --> 00:04:41,720
They're double walled, they're vacuum insulated.

53
00:04:41,721 --> 00:04:44,660
If you look real close, you can see the liquid nitrogen

54
00:04:44,661 --> 00:04:46,560
in this tank, it looks just like water

55
00:04:46,561 --> 00:04:48,840
except it's quite a bit colder.

56
00:04:48,841 --> 00:04:53,840
You'll notice that these semen canes are stored vertically.

57
00:04:54,100 --> 00:04:57,040
Five straws at the bottom, five straws at the top.

58
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:00,340
There's approximately 100,000 straws

59
00:05:00,341 --> 00:05:02,040
in each one of these large tanks.

60
00:05:03,920 --> 00:05:05,980
In the semen collection process,

61
00:05:05,981 --> 00:05:09,120
a bull is stimulated with surprising ease.

62
00:05:09,121 --> 00:05:13,260
simply walking behind a mount animal for a few seconds.

63
00:05:13,261 --> 00:05:19,620
The mount is another bull, a steer, that is a bull that has been castrated.

64
00:05:19,621 --> 00:05:23,940
A steer is used instead of a cow to prevent the bull from penetrating the other animal

65
00:05:23,941 --> 00:05:28,300
and picking up any sexually transmitted diseases.

66
00:05:28,301 --> 00:05:32,300
The collection technician will approach a bull that's already been stimulated.

67
00:05:32,301 --> 00:05:34,620
The bull will mount the mount animal.

68
00:05:34,621 --> 00:05:38,600
The technician will step forward and actually grab the bull's penis.

69
00:05:38,601 --> 00:05:41,640
He'll divert that penis into the collection apparatus.

70
00:05:41,641 --> 00:05:46,180
The bull will thrust, he'll ejaculate, and he'll return to the room with that collection

71
00:05:46,181 --> 00:05:53,420
equipment in hand and also a sample of semen contained inside it.

72
00:05:53,421 --> 00:05:57,960
New bulls learn how to go through this procedure by simply watching their more experienced

73
00:05:57,961 --> 00:05:59,400
counterparts.

74
00:05:59,401 --> 00:06:05,720
They're moaning and groaning, their ears are propped up, they're sniffing on each

75
00:06:05,721 --> 00:06:11,400
other sniffing the ground just they're actually being turned on by what they're actually seeing

76
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:19,480
the semen is immediately evaluated for density and motility to determine how many of the billions

77
00:06:19,481 --> 00:06:26,360
of sperm in each sample are alive and kicking it is then divided up into as many as 200 doses or

78
00:06:26,361 --> 00:06:28,960
or straws.

79
00:06:28,961 --> 00:06:34,640
Most straws sell for $15 to $25, but sometimes the price is much higher.

80
00:06:34,641 --> 00:06:41,640
Depending on the circumstances, we have seen semen sell for $1,000 or $2,000 a dose.

81
00:06:41,641 --> 00:06:47,760
That real expensive semen results from a bull that is a true breed changer and then passes

82
00:06:47,761 --> 00:06:51,040
away at an early, untimely period.

83
00:06:51,041 --> 00:06:56,460
There's a finite amount of genetic material on storage, and it sometimes turns into a

84
00:06:56,461 --> 00:07:03,960
bidding war just to get your hands on that last little bit of that special, special bull.

85
00:07:03,961 --> 00:07:09,980
Genetically desirable cows are also being subjected to state-of-the-art breeding techniques.

86
00:07:09,981 --> 00:07:14,680
This cow has received fertility drugs that triggered multiple ovulations.

87
00:07:14,681 --> 00:07:19,680
Artificial insemination resulted in a harvest of three dozen fertilized embryos, which are

88
00:07:19,681 --> 00:07:23,780
being flushed out through this tube.

89
00:07:23,781 --> 00:07:28,260
The embryos are carefully transferred to a sterilized dish.

90
00:07:28,261 --> 00:07:29,320
That's right.

91
00:07:29,321 --> 00:07:32,000
The living embryos of 36 head of cattle

92
00:07:32,001 --> 00:07:33,620
are floating in this Petri dish.

93
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:39,240
The embryos are examined under a microscope

94
00:07:39,241 --> 00:07:43,140
to make sure they're healthy, and then put inside an injection

95
00:07:43,141 --> 00:07:47,900
gun and implanted into receiver cows, who have been biologically

96
00:07:47,901 --> 00:07:53,360
synced up so that they are at the same stage of estrus as the ovulating cow and

97
00:07:53,361 --> 00:08:01,580
therefore ready to carry an embryo. The R.A. Brown Ranch is a seedstock ranch in

98
00:08:01,581 --> 00:08:06,080
Throckmorton, Texas. A seedstock ranch doesn't raise cattle for the slaughterhouse.

99
00:08:06,081 --> 00:08:11,000
Instead it develops superior bulls and cows that other ranches use to develop

100
00:08:11,001 --> 00:08:16,080
their herds. Most seedstock ranches today rely heavily on embryo transfers

101
00:08:16,081 --> 00:08:19,980
and artificial insemination in their quest for the ultimate bull and cow.

102
00:08:20,860 --> 00:08:25,720
We need to be five or ten years looking ahead to where the industry's going.

103
00:08:26,320 --> 00:08:31,560
And if we don't stay out on that cutting edge and be prepared for where the market's going to be,

104
00:08:31,680 --> 00:08:38,280
then we don't have the product to sell our customer to help him obtain the highest price he can get for his cattle.

105
00:08:39,280 --> 00:08:43,620
There is no natural selection between the cows and bulls on the Brown Ranch.

106
00:08:43,621 --> 00:08:51,620
incredibly every animal here is match mated to its ideal genetic partner the

107
00:08:51,621 --> 00:08:55,420
pairings are based on an extraordinary amount of data that the Browns maintain

108
00:08:55,421 --> 00:09:00,860
on their cattle each animal is weighed measured and sonogrammed at regular

109
00:09:00,861 --> 00:09:05,700
intervals to determine its all-important growth rates as well as the internal

110
00:09:05,701 --> 00:09:10,020
composition and marbling pattern of its flesh

111
00:09:10,021 --> 00:09:16,720
When they're a year old, the bulls are even measured for the size of their testicles.

112
00:09:16,721 --> 00:09:22,880
Testicular size in bulls has a direct relationship to semen quantity and quality.

113
00:09:22,881 --> 00:09:28,740
The bulls that have below 30-centimeter testicles at a year of age are then castrated and gone

114
00:09:28,741 --> 00:09:32,820
on to the feedlot to make beef.

115
00:09:32,821 --> 00:09:37,260
Detailed family trees on these animals are maintained through six generations.

116
00:09:37,261 --> 00:09:45,860
There is such a high correlation in the genetic makeup of an animal that they pass on to their progeny.

117
00:09:46,460 --> 00:09:52,500
So if our goal is to provide that superior protein, that nutritious, safe product for you,

118
00:09:52,640 --> 00:09:55,740
we've got to be able to get a handle on where it came from.

119
00:09:57,540 --> 00:10:04,780
All the numbers are crunched by computer to come up with an animal's expected progeny differences, or EPDs,

120
00:10:04,781 --> 00:10:08,380
which profile the kind of calves a cow or bull is likely to have.

121
00:10:09,960 --> 00:10:15,780
Evaluating EPDs and match-mating cattle are the core chores of cattle ranching at the Brown Ranch.

122
00:10:16,500 --> 00:10:22,400
I think people in the cattle industry don't even understand how much of a degree that we go to,

123
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:30,800
how many hours that my father-in-law and my husband spend identifying sire lines that have superb traits.

124
00:10:30,801 --> 00:10:40,900
They'll actually take an individual cow and look at what the calf might be if it were mated to this bull, this bull, and this bull.

125
00:10:40,980 --> 00:10:43,680
Three or four or five or ten or twenty different bulls.

126
00:10:43,820 --> 00:10:45,980
And then they'll choose the best combination.

127
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:52,960
The matings are diagrammed on a chalkboard in the ranch's artificial insemination barn.

128
00:10:54,260 --> 00:10:57,820
Semen from over 500 bulls is stored in the AI freeze tanks.

129
00:10:57,821 --> 00:11:04,820
We look in on our breeding sheets and figure out what bull that she has planned mated to.

130
00:11:04,821 --> 00:11:12,820
It's neat to see in a calf on the ground that you know that that's the cow that you AI'd

131
00:11:12,821 --> 00:11:19,820
that you're... it makes... you can just see what you've done.

132
00:11:19,821 --> 00:11:24,820
Such high-tech breeding is light years away from the ranching world of the 19th century cowboy.

133
00:11:24,821 --> 00:11:25,521
cowboy.

134
00:11:25,522 --> 00:11:31,220
Up next, the origins of cattle ranching in America and the legendary cattle drives.

135
00:11:32,780 --> 00:11:35,595
95 to 99 percent of all bulls are terminal.

136
00:11:35,596 --> 00:11:38,280
That is, they are castrated, fattened up,

137
00:11:38,420 --> 00:11:40,842
and sent to the slaughterhouse as adults.

138
00:11:40,843 --> 00:11:43,500
Cattle ranches will return on Modern Marvels.

139
00:11:48,060 --> 00:11:53,740
Cattle ranching in the United States, especially in the West, has always been famous for its

140
00:11:53,741 --> 00:11:57,140
spectacular spreads.

141
00:11:57,141 --> 00:12:00,540
But giant ranches no longer dominate the industry.

142
00:12:00,541 --> 00:12:05,812
Indeed only 55 of the more than 800,000 cattle operations in the U.S.

143
00:12:05,813 --> 00:12:07,340
have more than 5,000

144
00:12:07,341 --> 00:12:08,540
cows.

145
00:12:08,541 --> 00:12:14,500
And the average American rancher has less than 100 head of cattle.

146
00:12:14,501 --> 00:12:19,900
While Texas, Wyoming and a handful of other western states have major cattle industries,

147
00:12:19,901 --> 00:12:24,880
Fully half of our beef comes from east of the Mississippi, from farms where small herds

148
00:12:24,881 --> 00:12:31,200
of 25 to 50 cattle graze on land that once bloomed with cotton.

149
00:12:31,201 --> 00:12:36,140
Florida, with its heavy rainfall and rich grasses, has a tradition of cattle ranching

150
00:12:36,141 --> 00:12:39,300
that goes back to the 16th century.

151
00:12:39,301 --> 00:12:44,620
And Hawaii, of all places, is home to one of the largest cattle ranches in the U.S.

152
00:12:44,621 --> 00:12:52,300
225,000 acre Parker Ranch, which has been raising cattle since 1847.

153
00:12:52,301 --> 00:12:57,740
Humans first began domesticating cattle as a food source more than 6,500 years

154
00:12:57,741 --> 00:13:05,000
ago. The Egyptians were among the first to raise cattle for milk as well as meat

155
00:13:05,001 --> 00:13:12,120
and they kept careful records of their livestock as early as 2625 BC.

156
00:13:12,121 --> 00:13:16,120
Columbus brought cattle to the New World in 1494,

157
00:13:16,121 --> 00:13:19,120
and the Spanish introduced the Longhorn breed into Mexico,

158
00:13:19,121 --> 00:13:23,120
and eventually Texas, in 1598.

159
00:13:23,121 --> 00:13:27,120
The word ranch comes from the Spanish word rancho,

160
00:13:27,121 --> 00:13:30,120
which means home or headquarters.

161
00:13:30,121 --> 00:13:32,120
And it was the Spanish who brought the fundamentals

162
00:13:32,121 --> 00:13:35,120
of cattle ranching to the New World.

163
00:13:35,121 --> 00:13:37,120
On the one hand, they bring cattle to the New World,

164
00:13:37,121 --> 00:13:39,120
the source of food, if you will.

165
00:13:39,121 --> 00:13:42,260
if you will, they bring horses to the New World,

166
00:13:42,261 --> 00:13:44,720
and they also bring their culture to the New World.

167
00:13:44,721 --> 00:13:46,820
Spanish riding and herding traditions

168
00:13:46,821 --> 00:13:51,500
that come from Europe, from North Africa, and elsewhere

169
00:13:51,501 --> 00:13:53,740
are transported to the New World,

170
00:13:53,741 --> 00:13:55,160
and they spread from there.

171
00:13:56,400 --> 00:13:58,680
The Longhorn flourished in the rich grasslands

172
00:13:58,681 --> 00:14:00,940
of what would become Texas.

173
00:14:00,941 --> 00:14:02,740
And by the early 19th century,

174
00:14:02,741 --> 00:14:05,280
millions of cattle roamed the territory,

175
00:14:05,281 --> 00:14:08,080
free for anyone who could gather up a herd.

176
00:14:08,081 --> 00:14:12,740
Tom Saunders' great-great-grandfather brought his family to Texas in 1850.

177
00:14:13,200 --> 00:14:17,640
When they got to Gonzales County and started ranching, they got to catching cattle.

178
00:14:18,240 --> 00:14:22,600
You could just get as big as you wanted to get, or as big as you were tough enough to handle.

179
00:14:24,340 --> 00:14:28,100
During the Civil War, Texas supplied the Confederacy with beef.

180
00:14:28,320 --> 00:14:33,420
But when Northern armies took control of the Mississippi, Texas cattle had nowhere to go.

181
00:14:33,900 --> 00:14:35,580
The Longhorn population exploded.

182
00:14:35,581 --> 00:14:39,440
At one point, cattle outnumbered people in Texas eight to one.

183
00:14:42,240 --> 00:14:46,800
After the war, a meat-starved population drove the cattle market through the roof.

184
00:14:47,300 --> 00:14:51,300
A Texas steer worth $6 at home could fetch $40 in Kansas.

185
00:14:53,420 --> 00:15:00,220
Thus began the great and legendary cattle drives, a truly epic chapter in the history of American ranching.

186
00:15:01,140 --> 00:15:03,820
The cattle drive was a kind of ranch in motion.

187
00:15:03,821 --> 00:15:10,820
Typically it lasted three months and involved 12 to 15 cowboys who pushed a herd of 2,000

188
00:15:10,821 --> 00:15:16,820
to 3,000 cattle a thousand miles or more from south Texas to the railheads of the north.

189
00:15:16,821 --> 00:15:19,820
It really was an amazing feat.

190
00:15:19,821 --> 00:15:24,820
And eight miles a day was about an average of how far they could go.

191
00:15:24,821 --> 00:15:28,820
And so it took them months to get there.

192
00:15:28,821 --> 00:15:32,120
And of course in the spring, the rains would swell

193
00:15:32,121 --> 00:15:34,960
those old rivers and they'd get out of banks

194
00:15:34,961 --> 00:15:37,960
and a lot of lightning and thunder and so forth.

195
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:42,360
Roughing it to say the least was a way of life

196
00:15:42,361 --> 00:15:44,700
for cowboys on a drive.

197
00:15:44,701 --> 00:15:47,900
You might spend 24 hours horseback

198
00:15:47,901 --> 00:15:51,140
and a lot of them got good at sleeping in the saddle.

199
00:15:51,141 --> 00:15:55,780
Had to, if you hit dry country and it may be 15, 20 miles

200
00:15:55,781 --> 00:15:59,020
before you could hit good grass and water,

201
00:15:59,021 --> 00:16:01,780
then you had to push those cattle straight on through

202
00:16:01,781 --> 00:16:04,480
in one stretch.

203
00:16:05,900 --> 00:16:08,480
A rolling kitchen called the chuck wagon

204
00:16:08,481 --> 00:16:11,380
provided occasional creature comforts on the drive.

205
00:16:12,860 --> 00:16:15,780
The chuck wagon, usually driven by a cook

206
00:16:15,781 --> 00:16:17,140
or the cook's assistant,

207
00:16:17,600 --> 00:16:19,600
was sort of key to keeping the men happy.

208
00:16:19,820 --> 00:16:22,300
Men wanted to work for ranches that had the best cooks.

209
00:16:22,860 --> 00:16:25,340
The chuck wagon that was well-equipped with all the basics,

210
00:16:25,341 --> 00:16:32,540
beans, coffee, and flour for biscuits was an essential for the drive.

211
00:16:32,541 --> 00:16:39,020
Among the many dangers the cowboy faced, none was greater than that of the stampede.

212
00:16:39,021 --> 00:16:45,420
You're talking about nearly 300 years that these cattle ran wild down here all over

213
00:16:45,421 --> 00:16:50,350
this state. Any kind of an excuse, these cattle would break and run.

214
00:16:50,351 --> 00:16:52,380
I've been in one stampede in

215
00:16:52,381 --> 00:16:59,380
in Oklahoma and we had 400 and it sounded just like a freight train.

216
00:16:59,381 --> 00:17:03,180
And the ground was actually vibrate.

217
00:17:03,181 --> 00:17:08,180
So, you know, if you multiply that up into 2,500 running that away,

218
00:17:08,181 --> 00:17:11,980
it's hard to even comprehend what it's like.

219
00:17:11,981 --> 00:17:14,180
But yeah, it's a scary sucker.

220
00:17:14,181 --> 00:17:15,180
It's tough.

221
00:17:17,980 --> 00:17:21,180
What really spooked the Longhorn and the Cowboys

222
00:17:21,181 --> 00:17:24,980
was an eerie phenomenon known as St. Elmo's Fire.

223
00:17:24,981 --> 00:17:28,880
They generate so much heat in a herd.

224
00:17:28,881 --> 00:17:31,860
Well, when it's thundering and lightning,

225
00:17:31,861 --> 00:17:35,820
that heat draws that lightning,

226
00:17:35,821 --> 00:17:38,560
and it'd actually light up on these horns,

227
00:17:38,561 --> 00:17:40,140
and they'd just dance around.

228
00:17:40,141 --> 00:17:43,640
This blue fire just danced all over these horns.

229
00:17:43,641 --> 00:17:47,780
And you can imagine how that would scare you,

230
00:17:47,781 --> 00:17:49,480
your horse, and all these cattle.

231
00:17:51,180 --> 00:17:57,420
Overall, 10 million Texas cattle were driven to northern railheads on four major routes

232
00:17:57,421 --> 00:18:00,600
between 1865 and 1880.

233
00:18:00,601 --> 00:18:04,960
Kansas was the primary endpoint, but some herds went all the way to Canada.

234
00:18:04,961 --> 00:18:09,420
One reportedly grazed its way to New York.

235
00:18:09,421 --> 00:18:16,260
Tens of thousands of cowboys, earning between $10 to $20 a month, worked the trail drives,

236
00:18:16,261 --> 00:18:21,260
the skills, instincts, and lifestyle that would make them a national symbol.

237
00:18:21,261 --> 00:18:25,260
But they didn't always fit the popular 19th century stereotype.

238
00:18:25,261 --> 00:18:30,260
Between 15 and 30 percent of all cowboys were African American.

239
00:18:30,261 --> 00:18:34,260
Native Americans and Mexicans made up significant proportions as well.

240
00:18:34,261 --> 00:18:38,260
And with the exception of Texas, where carrying a gun was part of the culture,

241
00:18:38,261 --> 00:18:43,260
most cowboys didn't carry firearms, at least not while they worked.

242
00:18:43,261 --> 00:18:47,760
Guns won the West, certainly one of the statements that stays with us, but the

243
00:18:47,761 --> 00:18:51,540
fact is it really affected settlement of the West as far as the cowboy is

244
00:18:51,541 --> 00:18:57,540
concerned. It's not his gun, it's his raising of those cattle. Cattle ranchers

245
00:18:57,541 --> 00:19:01,040
would raise herds in ever greater numbers after the demise of the open

246
00:19:01,041 --> 00:19:09,260
range. Up next, barbed wire and the incredible ranching empires of the West.

247
00:19:09,261 --> 00:19:17,020
19th century cowboys had to use their 10 to 20 dollar per month salary to buy their own gear

248
00:19:17,021 --> 00:19:24,960
including a standard saddle that cost between 40 and 65 dollars cattle ranches will return

249
00:19:24,961 --> 00:19:36,220
on modern marvels in 1904 thomas edison recorded these classic scenes of cattle ranchers at work

250
00:19:36,221 --> 00:19:44,220
Rounding up the herd, roping, dragging, branding calves.

251
00:19:44,221 --> 00:19:51,640
Remarkably, just 20 years before Edison shot these scenes, cattle ranchers in the American

252
00:19:51,641 --> 00:19:57,640
West were still operating on the open range, grazing cattle almost entirely without fences.

253
00:19:57,641 --> 00:20:01,480
The open range had no boundaries.

254
00:20:01,481 --> 00:20:05,980
If you ran out of grass, water got short, you just got up and moved your herd somewhere

255
00:20:05,981 --> 00:20:08,380
and nobody would say anything different.

256
00:20:08,480 --> 00:20:09,480
It was great.

257
00:20:09,580 --> 00:20:11,580
I mean, I'd love to be able to, you know,

258
00:20:11,680 --> 00:20:13,780
look across the fence where my neighbor's not using this

259
00:20:13,880 --> 00:20:17,120
country and say, man, I could use that better than he could,

260
00:20:17,220 --> 00:20:19,920
but it don't work that way.

261
00:20:20,020 --> 00:20:22,320
With no fences to separate herds,

262
00:20:22,420 --> 00:20:25,040
branding played a crucial role in keeping track of who

263
00:20:25,140 --> 00:20:27,040
owned what cattle.

264
00:20:27,140 --> 00:20:31,180
Texas in the 1830s and 40s, brands were used to mark

265
00:20:31,280 --> 00:20:35,980
cattle usually on the left hip as to who they belonged to.

266
00:20:35,981 --> 00:20:41,620
The process in the spring of branding cattle was a basic part of the cowboys' work.

267
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:47,540
Like the old boy said, a brand is really the only return address on this cow,

268
00:20:48,760 --> 00:20:50,780
because you know where she came from.

269
00:20:52,160 --> 00:20:56,860
Richard Chisholm came up with the first American brand in Texas in 1832.

270
00:20:57,900 --> 00:21:00,340
Another early Texas rancher, Samuel Maverick,

271
00:21:00,940 --> 00:21:04,300
became famous for his inability to brand his wild longhorn.

272
00:21:05,060 --> 00:21:07,800
The unbranded cattle became known as Mavericks.

273
00:21:10,200 --> 00:21:13,600
Branding was not, however, a foolproof way of identifying cattle.

274
00:21:14,540 --> 00:21:17,340
Cattle rustlers specialized in ways of altering brands.

275
00:21:18,060 --> 00:21:21,560
If you had a simple bee brand for your last initial,

276
00:21:22,140 --> 00:21:26,380
you could put a curved mark below that so it became the rocking bee,

277
00:21:26,381 --> 00:21:29,680
or you could put a straight mark below it so it became the bar bee.

278
00:21:29,681 --> 00:21:36,880
needless to say rustlers operated at their own peril as charlie goodnight a legendary texas

279
00:21:36,881 --> 00:21:42,500
rancher liked to point out gee you see there has been a few rustlers that hung around here

280
00:21:42,501 --> 00:21:49,720
but they didn't hang for long so they strung them with a rope as soon as they caught them

281
00:21:49,721 --> 00:21:56,920
brand books which were widely circulated in the 1880s were crucial in enabling ranchers to verify

282
00:21:56,921 --> 00:22:03,520
ownership. This one from Montana in 1886 identified brand, breed, and location of

283
00:22:03,521 --> 00:22:07,600
the brand on the animal for hundreds of herds.

284
00:22:09,120 --> 00:22:15,100
The open rain shut down in the 1880s as severe winters wiped out wandering

285
00:22:15,101 --> 00:22:20,380
herds and as the railroad extended further west and south making the cattle

286
00:22:20,381 --> 00:22:26,080
drive unnecessary. In addition, cattle drivers flush with cash after selling

287
00:22:26,081 --> 00:22:29,280
their herds had begun buying up the land.

288
00:22:29,281 --> 00:22:32,820
In Texas, especially, the rich pasture was dirt cheap.

289
00:22:33,960 --> 00:22:37,960
This whole country is bringing 12, 14 cents an acre

290
00:22:37,961 --> 00:22:42,260
and the guys that had any money was buying more of it.

291
00:22:42,261 --> 00:22:44,500
And of course, the land started going up.

292
00:22:45,700 --> 00:22:47,440
Charlie Goodnight thought it was terrible

293
00:22:47,441 --> 00:22:50,400
when it got up to $1.50 an acre.

294
00:22:50,401 --> 00:22:53,640
Thought that was the most outrageous thing it ever was.

295
00:22:53,641 --> 00:23:00,640
At 12 cents an acre, $1,200 got you 10,000 acres.

296
00:23:00,641 --> 00:23:06,640
At $1.50 an acre, that $1,200 only got you 800 acres.

297
00:23:06,641 --> 00:23:11,640
The 825,000 acre King Ranch in Kingsville, Texas,

298
00:23:11,641 --> 00:23:14,640
which is still intact and larger than the state of Rhode Island,

299
00:23:14,641 --> 00:23:18,640
was one of the first and greatest ranching empires.

300
00:23:18,641 --> 00:23:20,640
It's our own world.

301
00:23:20,641 --> 00:23:24,340
The men that worked the land was brought out of Mexico

302
00:23:24,440 --> 00:23:27,880
by Captain King, and he needed some hands,

303
00:23:27,980 --> 00:23:31,020
and he went down there and moved a whole village

304
00:23:31,120 --> 00:23:32,260
of Mexican people.

305
00:23:32,360 --> 00:23:34,420
He said, I want every one of you,

306
00:23:34,520 --> 00:23:37,320
and you all just come up here and work for us.

307
00:23:37,420 --> 00:23:42,620
And their descendants are still there today.

308
00:23:42,720 --> 00:23:46,200
Giant ranches wouldn't have meant much without fencing.

309
00:23:46,300 --> 00:23:49,700
Until the 1870s, fencing made of wood or stone

310
00:23:49,701 --> 00:23:56,740
too expensive to use over a large area. Barbed wire introduced in 1873

311
00:23:56,741 --> 00:24:01,940
revolutionized cattle ranching. If we didn't have barbed wire, certainly

312
00:24:01,941 --> 00:24:05,780
something else would have to have developed and it's unlikely that ranching

313
00:24:05,781 --> 00:24:09,620
as we know it would have been possible. There was a great industry in creating

314
00:24:09,621 --> 00:24:14,500
different patents for different designs of barbed wire so that a few patents go

315
00:24:14,501 --> 00:24:18,900
into a few dozen patents go into a few hundred patents so that over time there

316
00:24:18,901 --> 00:24:21,940
have been at least a couple of thousand variations of barbed wire.

317
00:24:21,941 --> 00:24:27,660
When it was first introduced, barbed wire was a tough sell, as an early patent holder

318
00:24:27,661 --> 00:24:29,660
named Joseph Gliddens found out.

319
00:24:29,661 --> 00:24:36,100
Nobody believed that a little old wire would hold them old big longhorn cattle and it was

320
00:24:36,101 --> 00:24:37,900
just a waste of money and a waste of time.

321
00:24:37,901 --> 00:24:45,040
So he put this barbed wire around this pen and he invited everybody to bring the wildest

322
00:24:45,041 --> 00:24:50,080
cattle and he guaranteed they couldn't run them through this fence. And they don't.

323
00:24:50,081 --> 00:24:53,920
A bunch of them boys got in and got to hooping and hollering and popping bull

324
00:24:53,921 --> 00:24:59,020
whips and everything and they couldn't make these steers, couldn't get them through.

325
00:24:59,021 --> 00:25:04,700
They even took torches at night and tried to run them through it. And that's how he

326
00:25:04,701 --> 00:25:10,700
started selling barbed wire. Fencing the range profoundly transformed cattle

327
00:25:10,701 --> 00:25:12,100
ranching in America.

328
00:25:12,101 --> 00:25:16,720
In a sense, an age of innocence was over, an age in which ranching

329
00:25:16,721 --> 00:25:21,020
had been pretty much a matter of letting the cattle themselves do the job, eat their fill

330
00:25:21,021 --> 00:25:26,640
of free grass and reproduce themselves in large numbers. With fences, ranchers could

331
00:25:26,641 --> 00:25:31,120
begin thinking about improving the quality and even the genetic makeup of their herds.

332
00:25:32,600 --> 00:25:38,600
Almost immediately, new breeds were introduced, less hardy but tastier European breeds,

333
00:25:38,601 --> 00:25:44,360
like the Angus and the Hereford. Some ranches began creating their own breeds,

334
00:25:44,361 --> 00:25:49,760
ideally suited to a specific market and terrain. King Ranch introduced the

335
00:25:49,761 --> 00:25:54,800
Santa Gertrudis in 1920, a cross between the Brahman and the Shorthorn, and the

336
00:25:54,801 --> 00:26:00,980
first new breed of cattle to emerge in North America. Fences also made it

337
00:26:00,981 --> 00:26:05,300
possible to begin to manage the mating of livestock, using the best bulls to

338
00:26:05,301 --> 00:26:08,400
replenish the herd.

339
00:26:08,401 --> 00:26:11,300
Seed stock ranches, which specialized in raising

340
00:26:11,301 --> 00:26:14,680
genetically superior bulls for sale to other ranches,

341
00:26:14,681 --> 00:26:18,220
emerged in the 1920s.

342
00:26:18,221 --> 00:26:21,380
With the death of the open range, the push was on to

343
00:26:21,381 --> 00:26:24,720
improve cattle, make them bigger, meatier, hardier,

344
00:26:24,721 --> 00:26:29,720
tastier, and more profitable than ever.

345
00:26:29,721 --> 00:26:33,100
Most of all, 20th century cattle in the U.S.

346
00:26:33,101 --> 00:26:36,540
would have to learn how to gain weight and fast.

347
00:26:36,541 --> 00:26:40,340
Up next, where cattle go for a final phase of feasting

348
00:26:40,341 --> 00:26:41,800
before the slaughterhouse.

349
00:26:46,140 --> 00:26:49,980
By 1927, the Texas Longhorn was an endangered species

350
00:26:49,981 --> 00:26:53,320
with less than 200 left in the United States.

351
00:26:53,321 --> 00:26:55,280
But the Longhorn has made a comeback

352
00:26:55,281 --> 00:26:59,920
and now has a population of over 250,000.

353
00:26:59,921 --> 00:27:02,480
Cattle ranches will return on Modern Marvels.

354
00:27:06,640 --> 00:27:10,140
Cattle ranching with its cowboys, horses, and rangeland

355
00:27:10,141 --> 00:27:11,160
has a great image.

356
00:27:11,880 --> 00:27:14,440
But the same can hardly be said of red meat.

357
00:27:16,440 --> 00:27:18,860
Meat has been on the defensive for decades,

358
00:27:19,340 --> 00:27:21,580
first because of health concerns about animal fat,

359
00:27:21,860 --> 00:27:25,000
and more recently because of alarming outbreaks of E. coli,

360
00:27:25,140 --> 00:27:26,960
foot and mouth, and mad cow disease.

361
00:27:29,920 --> 00:27:35,140
In addition, animal rights activists have assailed the meat industry for alleged cruelty to cattle,

362
00:27:36,080 --> 00:27:42,620
while vegetarians claim that eating meat is wasteful, environmentally unsound, and even immoral.

363
00:27:44,320 --> 00:27:48,260
The inconsistency of meat has also given the industry a bad name.

364
00:27:49,680 --> 00:27:57,280
There's been a lot of research that's been done that has shown that one out of four beef-eating experiences is a bad one,

365
00:27:57,600 --> 00:27:58,840
relative to tenderness.

366
00:27:58,841 --> 00:28:02,140
and that's too high. Those odds are no good for us.

367
00:28:03,660 --> 00:28:06,840
Cattle ranchers who want to stay afloat in the 21st century

368
00:28:06,841 --> 00:28:09,980
are extremely serious about reorganizing their industry

369
00:28:09,981 --> 00:28:13,700
with efficiency and consistency of product uppermost in mind.

370
00:28:15,580 --> 00:28:18,000
To that end, some have banded together

371
00:28:18,001 --> 00:28:21,640
in hopes of winning back consumers with an actual brand of beef

372
00:28:21,641 --> 00:28:22,980
that comes with a promise.

373
00:28:23,700 --> 00:28:25,660
The promise is we're guaranteeing tenderness,

374
00:28:25,661 --> 00:28:31,840
It's verified tender product, and it's a result of a lot of hard work that we've put together

375
00:28:31,841 --> 00:28:35,380
tracking the cattle all the way through the entire system.

376
00:28:37,320 --> 00:28:42,860
To deliver on that guarantee, ranchers have to evaluate their cattle from conception to carcass

377
00:28:42,861 --> 00:28:46,280
in order literally to recreate the American cow.

378
00:28:48,020 --> 00:28:53,200
Through genetic weeding and selection, ranchers hope to create a kind of cow light

379
00:28:53,201 --> 00:28:55,640
with less fat on the outside of the steak,

380
00:28:55,960 --> 00:28:58,020
but good interior marbling and flavor,

381
00:28:58,820 --> 00:29:01,160
a cow that delivers a consistent piece of meat,

382
00:29:01,580 --> 00:29:06,080
a uniform, standard cow that has the perfect traits for efficient ranching.

383
00:29:07,780 --> 00:29:12,500
The ideal type of cow is going to be one that makes money,

384
00:29:12,840 --> 00:29:15,060
and that can happen from a bunch of different ways.

385
00:29:16,620 --> 00:29:19,300
Indeed, ranchers have taken away so much fat

386
00:29:19,301 --> 00:29:21,620
and added so much meat to the American cow

387
00:29:21,621 --> 00:29:26,540
that we're producing more pounds of beef today than we did in the mid-1970s,

388
00:29:26,880 --> 00:29:29,420
with about 20 million fewer head of cattle.

389
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:34,880
As much as anything, cattle in today's American ranching business

390
00:29:34,881 --> 00:29:37,740
have to be good at eating and great at gaining weight,

391
00:29:38,040 --> 00:29:41,240
which is why three-quarters of all U.S. beef cattle

392
00:29:41,241 --> 00:29:44,800
spend the last few months of their lives being finished,

393
00:29:44,801 --> 00:29:48,480
to use the terminology of the trade, on a feedlot.

394
00:29:51,620 --> 00:30:00,480
In this feedlot in Cactus, Texas, 80,000 cattle are segregated by breed, age, and weight into 420 pens.

395
00:30:02,880 --> 00:30:12,020
Feedlots have emerged as a major sector of the cattle industry because feedlot cattle, which eat corn and grains instead of grass, put on pounds at twice the rate of

396
00:30:12,021 --> 00:30:12,740
ranch cattle.

397
00:30:13,800 --> 00:30:17,520
It actually increased the overall supply of beef.

398
00:30:17,521 --> 00:30:21,340
If it weren't for the feed yards and the feeding industry in the United States today,

399
00:30:21,341 --> 00:30:23,420
we would literally have a beef shortage.

400
00:30:23,620 --> 00:30:29,320
We're almost doubling the pounds of beef produced through the feed yard operations.

401
00:30:31,140 --> 00:30:36,960
In addition to bulking up quickly, grain-fed cattle also tastes better, according to feedlot operators.

402
00:30:37,620 --> 00:30:42,380
There's typically more marbling, which is the fat that's inside a nice steak

403
00:30:42,381 --> 00:30:46,700
that gives it the juiciness and the flavor that most consumers like.

404
00:30:47,520 --> 00:30:54,520
There are 700 major feedlots in the United States, mostly in the Southwest and Midwest.

405
00:30:54,521 --> 00:31:04,520
Cactus Feeders, a Fortune 500 company that operates nine yards in Texas and Kansas, is the largest feedlot conglomerate in the world.

406
00:31:04,521 --> 00:31:09,520
We have a one-time capacity of 460,000 head.

407
00:31:09,521 --> 00:31:14,920
We turn that inventory about two and a half times a year, so you can easily see where

408
00:31:14,921 --> 00:31:19,340
we feed in excess of a million cattle a year.

409
00:31:19,341 --> 00:31:24,920
The first feedlots appeared in the Midwest in the 1950s, and they served two purposes,

410
00:31:24,921 --> 00:31:29,280
as a feeding pit stop for cattle en route to the slaughterhouse, and as a way of getting

411
00:31:29,281 --> 00:31:32,040
rid of surplus corn.

412
00:31:32,041 --> 00:31:38,920
By the 1960s, feedlots had evolved into sophisticated fattening farms, and to this day, nutritionists

413
00:31:38,921 --> 00:31:45,380
and veterinarians keep searching for the ultimate high-gain, low-cost diet.

414
00:31:45,381 --> 00:31:49,560
The cost-to-gain ratio is the key measure of feed yard efficiency.

415
00:31:49,561 --> 00:31:55,960
In other words, how much food does it take for a cow to gain a single pound?

416
00:31:55,961 --> 00:32:01,400
The answer varies from breed to breed, but on average, cattle eat between 25 and 30 pounds

417
00:32:01,401 --> 00:32:07,840
of feed a day, which results in a daily weight gain of 2 to 3 pounds.

418
00:32:07,841 --> 00:32:12,960
Every morning, feed engineers inspect each pen to see how well the cattle are gaining.

419
00:32:12,961 --> 00:32:17,440
They check out their feed history by computer and order up feed assignments for the day.

420
00:32:17,441 --> 00:32:23,440
You can draw up on that laptop computer not only what that pen ate yesterday, but he can

421
00:32:23,441 --> 00:32:29,160
even have a 21-day determination or showing on that computer what it ate.

422
00:32:29,161 --> 00:32:35,240
All this provides him with a guide on how many pounds of feed that he'd like to have

423
00:32:35,241 --> 00:32:42,520
feed truck delivered to that particular pen. A feed mill grinds out most of the

424
00:32:42,521 --> 00:32:47,060
rations used on the cactus lot. Two million pounds worth of corn, hay and

425
00:32:47,061 --> 00:32:56,340
supplements every day. A cactus research wing conducts daily drug and feeding

426
00:32:56,341 --> 00:33:01,280
tests on some 10,000 cattle. These new arrivals are receiving experimental

427
00:33:01,281 --> 00:33:05,520
hormone growth implants, which are stapled into their ear.

428
00:33:05,521 --> 00:33:11,040
Typically, what you'll get with an implant like we're looking at is a response, oh, anywhere

429
00:33:11,041 --> 00:33:17,600
from maybe 10 to 15 percent improvement in feed efficiency or average daily gain compared

430
00:33:17,601 --> 00:33:21,900
to what you'd have with a non-implanted animal.

431
00:33:21,901 --> 00:33:26,440
The use of growth hormones is one of the most controversial practices in cattle ranching.

432
00:33:26,441 --> 00:33:36,580
It's never been proved that there's ever been a residue, a hormone residue in beef, as a result of using these growth promoters.

433
00:33:37,340 --> 00:33:41,180
They're extremely important from the standpoint of lowering our cost of production.

434
00:33:43,580 --> 00:33:49,540
The hormones are all FDA approved, but many consumers are concerned about possible side effects.

435
00:33:50,200 --> 00:33:53,840
European countries have banned the import of American beef that has hormones.

436
00:33:53,841 --> 00:34:00,020
feedlots have also been under fire for environmental reasons the main complaint

437
00:34:00,021 --> 00:34:09,740
toxic runoff from the manure that piles up here 80,000 head of cattle eating 25 to 30 pounds of

438
00:34:09,741 --> 00:34:18,680
feed a day will generate a lot of manure and the runoff can seep into the water table at cactus

439
00:34:18,681 --> 00:34:25,680
the runoff is collected and dumped into lagoons, then recycled to fertilize 2,000 acres of feedlot farmland.

440
00:34:28,440 --> 00:34:33,820
Meanwhile, about half of the dry manure is composted and distributed free to local farmers.

441
00:34:35,760 --> 00:34:39,060
Manure management inside the pens themselves is also important.

442
00:34:39,840 --> 00:34:43,440
As the dung accumulates, cactus penhands pile it up into mounds.

443
00:34:43,441 --> 00:34:51,680
When we do have heavy snowfall or rain and so forth, which creates mud, these mounds

444
00:34:51,681 --> 00:34:57,740
dry very quickly and it gives the animals a dry place to lay down.

445
00:34:57,741 --> 00:35:01,000
Cattle in muddy conditions do not gain well.

446
00:35:01,001 --> 00:35:08,600
It raises the cost of gain, efficiency goes down, mainly because the cattle are very uncomfortable.

447
00:35:08,601 --> 00:35:14,680
We want these cattle to be as comfortable as we possibly can have it.

448
00:35:14,681 --> 00:35:19,980
The slaughterhouse, of course, is the last stop in the cattle ranching story.

449
00:35:19,981 --> 00:35:25,280
The cattle are killed with a bolt gun that punches a retractable rod into their forehead.

450
00:35:25,281 --> 00:35:29,660
99% of the animal is used commercially.

451
00:35:29,661 --> 00:35:35,620
Cattle byproducts are in everything from tires to asphalt to drywall.

452
00:35:35,621 --> 00:35:38,000
Slaughtering isn't the only thing that takes place here.

